# Migration SQL is checksummed by sqlx over the raw file bytes, and the checksum
# recorded in a production database is immutable. With core.autocrlf=true on
# Windows, these files were checked out with CRLF and the shipped image carried
# CRLF, so 20260625045653 was recorded that way.
#
# 20261028000000 was applied from an image that carried LF, so it is recorded
# as LF. The two disagree, and the only thing that satisfies both is pinning
# each file's line endings explicitly rather than letting autocrlf decide.
#
# Do not rewrite either migration. If one must change, the appended form
# (ALTER/CREATE that is idempotent) is the way, not an edit to these bytes.
migrations/20260625045653_photos*.sql            text eol=crlf
migrations/20261028000000_photo_status*.sql      text eol=lf

# Source and templates are read by the compiler and Tera, which do not care,
# but normalising them keeps diffs from churning on Windows.
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
